--TEST--
every arithmetic and bitwise operator refuses an enum operand, whether the enum is nullable, a single case or plain, because a case is not a number — `rule:types/arithmetic`, `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
enum Size: int { Small = 1, Large = 2 }

Size $s = Size::Small;
?Size $n = 2 as ?Size;
Size::Large $only = Size::Large as Size::Large;

// A prefix operator on a plain enum value.
mixed $a = -$s;
// A binary operator on a case written in place.
mixed $b = Size::Small + 1;
// `as` binds tighter than prefix `-`, so this is `-(1 as ?Size)`: a minus on a nullable enum.
mixed $c = -1 as ?Size;
// A nullable enum is refused on either side of a binary operator.
mixed $d = 2 * $n;
// The bitwise operators refuse it the same way.
mixed $e = ~$n;
// A variable whose type is one case is still an enum value.
mixed $f = $only | 1;
// `++` is `+ 1`, so it is refused too.
$n++;
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0415]: `-` has no meaning for `Size`
%A
error[E0415]: `+` has no meaning for `Size`
%A
error[E0415]: `-` has no meaning for `null|Size`
%A
error[E0415]: `*` has no meaning for `null|Size`
%A
error[E0415]: `~` has no meaning for `null|Size`
%A
error[E0415]: `|` has no meaning for `Size::Large`
%A
error[E0415]: `++`/`--` has no meaning for `null|Size`
%A
